<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Chatti</h1><p class="st-meta">10 BCE – 260 CE (approx.) · capital Mattium</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A disciplined Germanic people of Hesse who resisted Rome for two centuries and are often seen as ancestors of the Hessians.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Chatti lived in the hill country of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda-river">Fulda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eder-river">Eder</a>, roughly modern northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. They appear in Roman sources around 10 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero-claudius-drusus">Drusus</a> campaigned against them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> burned their chief centre, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mattium">Mattium</a>, in 15 CE, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> never brought them under lasting control.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> describes the Chatti as unusually well organised for Germans: they chose leaders, kept ranks, entrenched camps and planned campaigns. Young warriors wore an iron ring and left their hair uncut until they had killed an enemy. They farmed the fertile Hessian basins and fought regular wars with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hermunduri">Hermunduri</a> over salt springs.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a> fought the Chatti in 83 to 85 and pushed the frontier forward along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taunus">Taunus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wetterau">Wetterau</a>, the line later fortified as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limes-germanicus">Limes</a>. After about 260 they appear among the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/franks">Franks</a>. Their name is generally linked to the later Hessians, though the connection is debated.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chatti.jpg" alt="Chatti" width="320" height="162"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Lancaster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lower_Rhine_10BC.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>10 BCE – 260 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mattium</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Germanic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Weser-Rhine Germanic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hessians</dd></dl></aside></div>
